#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} \n&= \\lim_{x\\to3}  \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 3}  \\dfrac {3x^2}{1}\\[5pt]\n&=  3(3)^2\\[5pt]\n&= 27\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate  $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\n}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac{0}{0}.$ But that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'}\\[5pt]\n&=  2\\lim_{x\\to0}  \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1}\\[5pt]\n&= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\\n&=4.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} .$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n$\\displaystyle\n \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty$ and $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty$ respectively,\nso we have the case $\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}$.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)}\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= \\lim_{x\\to 0}  \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3} }\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}  \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2}\\[5pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0\\[5pt]\n&= 0.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4} }.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty},$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty}  \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'}\\\n&= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0}\\\n&=  \\lim_{x\\to \\infty}  \\dfrac {1}{5}\\\n&= \\dfrac{1}{5}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f236ca-0647-5109-83ea-2610f579e1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286511} {:answer/id #uuid "8b716572-c2cd-567a-8b2b-5d311f0d6a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286512} {:answer/id #uuid "4d72059e-10df-5094-bd80-107ecbac93b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286513} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b22b3b-3db7-56d9-ae03-146b2ceba316" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286514} {:answer/id #uuid "9a96ed51-8269-5255-9554-ed9bf372f0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286515}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f236ca-0647-5109-83ea-2610f579e1bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286511} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a90530-42e9-5508-bcaa-14eff4ccaf5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d78ff30-662b-5fb9-981a-44d909d0721b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114249" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}+2x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)-x-1}{{x}^{2}+2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]}^{′}}{{[2x+2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x+2})}{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{1}{2(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2922593b-f08f-513a-a291-4e6e39a50861" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382733} {:answer/id #uuid "76bbb6ff-a567-53d8-a9ef-bfda58cd60c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382734} {:answer/id #uuid "7a58d85f-54b0-5066-8718-27b52202e334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382735} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d0917b-d5e5-5853-803a-4431540b20b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382736} {:answer/id #uuid "c892f45c-edec-52aa-9d8e-808acbc0e409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c892f45c-edec-52aa-9d8e-808acbc0e409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382737} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc26de5f-8ba5-5290-8c5c-e1212472baa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a04167c-7a25-5d39-ae6d-b1e4e325783d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115519" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(x+1)=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x}{x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-x)-2x]^{′}}{{[x+1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{1-x}-2)}{1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{1-x}+2) \\\\  & =-(0+2) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4617dc05-b7c3-54a3-8ba8-402846b15bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296240} {:answer/id #uuid "fa083fa1-257f-52c1-8bdd-4e16083cfdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296241} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5df6a8-9107-5e7c-a593-62f2641b4f68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296242} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fa7d81-a547-5b0b-86d2-58082d6e4845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296243} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cb5d759-46ce-5e4f-9224-43fce07f63e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296244} :answer-field/id #uuid "11cbad53-4033-52c7-8499-d026641bafc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c54e2735-245d-5798-9c48-ef40ec1547e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31253" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-6}{{x}^{2}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{2x+1}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2)+1}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a9fa45-1fd9-5ad8-baf0-6c183eceedb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358194} {:answer/id #uuid "cd42b75e-0b19-54aa-8e37-03971691861e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358195} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04da96-5dc5-5fd8-909d-c920648a3dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358196} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca95696-6501-56b4-8be4-3d4850c2c883" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358197} {:answer/id #uuid "ed48adbf-80fe-5551-9fba-eac4ec9e48e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd04da96-5dc5-5fd8-909d-c920648a3dd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358196} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ebccb2-4065-5774-bee3-5949510d5f65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f6b45e4-aad3-52ce-a5c4-dc96f14dd157" :question/math-academy-id "q-31272" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\sqrt{x+4}-x-8}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x+4}}-1]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(x+4)\\sqrt{x+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{(0+4)\\sqrt{0+4}}]}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{[-\\frac{1}{4(2)}]}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae08211b-5a62-538e-956c-9c52bfee2b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382740} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6bf656-cece-5d31-a6ed-d9739229f227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c6bf656-cece-5d31-a6ed-d9739229f227" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382741} :answer-field/id #uuid "becb05f3-39c9-5272-bcab-cefeae7afcad" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c867c3ac-b233-50f9-a7e6-dcc7e9a5b277" :question/math-academy-id "q-314141" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{7{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}-9}{10-3x-7{x}^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{7{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}-9}{10-3x-7{x}^{3}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[7{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}-9]^{′}}{[10-3x-7{x}^{3}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{28{x}^{3}+4x}{-3-21{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{28(1)^{3}+4(1)}{-3-21(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{28+4}{-3-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{(-24)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "975a2b7b-f0d4-596a-9ae7-9c1ffed9b7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382744} {:answer/id #uuid "1f997f2c-5557-55bd-b731-4b16a4585873" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "975a2b7b-f0d4-596a-9ae7-9c1ffed9b7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382744} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0b4a3fe-cd2b-575e-938e-063e953635ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "27356911-a306-56f7-a4fc-02e38fb05909" :question/math-academy-id "q-336777" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1)}{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}+1]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-4}}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de1fde5-b622-518b-ad82-82149805110c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3de1fde5-b622-518b-ad82-82149805110c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382748} :answer-field/id #uuid "18b2c6a1-89fb-570b-8112-700a06af6483" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fbc02c6-b501-5eec-8b59-c16ce48b0e6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336790" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡x]}^{′}}{{[{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382749 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7f9b1ad-6574-5f0a-98e2-03b07773efc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7f9b1ad-6574-5f0a-98e2-03b07773efc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382751} :answer-field/id #uuid "429278f0-2f1d-561f-b7b3-f0620da95645" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b1ee7cf-14c4-52ed-a4c9-8bd4dbb68e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-336899" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{′}}{{[({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2x-2}{4x({x}^{2}-9)+32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{2{x}^{3}-18x+16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{2}-2x+1}{({x}^{2}-9)^{2}+32x-96} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x-1}{2{x}^{3}-18x+16} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{[x-1]}^{′}}{{[2{x}^{3}-18x+16]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{6{x}^{2}-18} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6(1)^{2}-18} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea25ec-db6b-58be-a50e-d5eac4a24611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b65e21-4124-599c-8ef5-38bd7add3afa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e257b16a-a775-5397-9d95-12edd8b09eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336903" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{′}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{′}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "506d9697-ea2a-524d-a154-f90f148c8016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "506d9697-ea2a-524d-a154-f90f148c8016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382754} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc93b39c-eb23-566a-930e-a415dee59caf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f590b133-99f7-591b-b6c9-d85e4d4334e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336939" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{5}{1+5x}-5)}{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+5x)-5x}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{1+5x}-1)}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[(1+5x)^{-1}-1]}^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-5(1+5x)^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(-5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266154} :answer-field/id #uuid "445ecb86-4291-52cd-9f90-72e378f6a2ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1391e51-b3fc-5d6d-af4b-284d914341dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336951" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f695ca6-76e6-5a28-b810-7fc56c04ff32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382757} {:answer/id #uuid "3c42912c-d1b4-5c7a-8839-77c7522e4cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f695ca6-76e6-5a28-b810-7fc56c04ff32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b8aa0da-cf0d-5f4a-9baf-094168258a5a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38d3cf83-bd89-5be8-bd8f-dc1bce7bfe18" :question/math-academy-id "q-336956" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x+\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3x+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})}{\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6\\sqrt{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8bd0a17-4706-5928-8600-0383bcf572b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8bd0a17-4706-5928-8600-0383bcf572b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382761} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4c0221-1b1c-59d0-9d62-8285e27c9a0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2d95c01-9555-57f8-b02b-000768fa79cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336967" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}}{2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}+\\sqrt{x}}{{x}^{2}-5\\sqrt{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{x}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}}{{[2x-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{-1/2}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/2}}{2+\\frac{5}{4}{x}^{-3/2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-\\frac{1}{4{x}^{3/2}}}{2+\\frac{5}{4{x}^{3/2}}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} {:answer/id #uuid "4919d0de-8116-5b72-91dd-335bf99cd522" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296277}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c44539-981e-5a81-92d0-f00213c9265c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296276} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0cacba6-2b91-5aed-b8c1-d516321b9e10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb212d02-e01a-5a05-96a9-bd2df6014b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336971" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3}]^{′}}{[{ln}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{3}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[3{x}^{3}]^{′}}{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{9{x}^{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e283ae6-1616-570f-913b-aae850a87c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382763} {:answer/id #uuid "feaec291-80df-573e-ae81-cf13bb3463fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382764} {:answer/id #uuid "44b0146a-6324-57fa-838e-8f14441ef345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382765} {:answer/id #uuid "f9adf58f-c535-5f00-b5bf-a1f28761852b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382766} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4b4ddf-93fc-5ce8-b74d-18aa53d3e83d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e283ae6-1616-570f-913b-aae850a87c4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382763} :answer-field/id #uuid "425eb2ff-60c8-5fb2-8d6e-fe901807b6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe552b19-ecf8-5441-b376-c5bd68d19d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-3771" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{3x}-1}{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{3x}-1]^{′}}{[x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{e}^{3x}}{1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\  & =3(1) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382768 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9095015-a714-58c6-a9c8-666c90d50069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382770} {:answer/id #uuid "0a492da3-244e-53e9-8fc1-0a82171cc58c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382771} {:answer/id #uuid "cb035e8f-016b-5e19-bf2b-3c46ffe24954" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382772} {:answer/id #uuid "2ced3304-7ac4-50f7-b4da-3f2fd40f375b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382773} {:answer/id #uuid "07ed3234-46d2-5b36-9370-76bf480310fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07ed3234-46d2-5b36-9370-76bf480310fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382774} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb657fe6-a6b6-552c-89eb-f7d5167bedb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382769}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "523acc35-7db0-52db-b989-53904d8cc74c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3772" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{x-1}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{4}-1}{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[{x}^{4}-1]^{′}}{[x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{1} \\\\  & =4(1)^{3} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e478017-da1c-50b7-b8f6-542e441315f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382777} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d22797-68e4-5934-9fe2-d72bbcf6948b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382778} {:answer/id #uuid "98deb442-25e0-5ec9-8aaf-ff7517624e49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382779} {:answer/id #uuid "27077123-d6bf-5e36-84fd-a92c596518ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382780} {:answer/id #uuid "6bdba9aa-c9c8-5a2b-b7fe-cecaef001e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e478017-da1c-50b7-b8f6-542e441315f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382777} :answer-field/id #uuid "100864cd-b156-5ffb-8a5c-afe30dd60eb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4b9637a9-77cc-51e7-9441-fe892f3b865b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3774" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-3x-14}{{x}^{2}+x-6}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{5{x}^{2}-3x-14}{{x}^{2}+x-6} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{[5{x}^{2}-3x-14]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x-6]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{10x-3}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(2)-3}{2(2)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd470956-f46a-524f-93f6-7cfea4f61dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} {:answer/id #uuid "cad86e4c-2b0f-506d-8803-4288769136a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275731} {:answer/id #uuid "653ab907-40cd-5543-b0cd-e07538abe574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275732} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0555b1-3de4-5f74-b04d-e9711990e5ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275733} {:answer/id #uuid "32d005f8-8c9d-5b1c-a2d5-30ae5e4fe9cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd470956-f46a-524f-93f6-7cfea4f61dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275730} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b9f4c3-b07d-5a7b-a386-ed4be8f82892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5460231d-5ab3-5dda-afe5-f1c42598ed15" :question/math-academy-id "q-72335" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}}{(\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}})} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4{e}^{2/{x}^{2}}\\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}}{\\cancel{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})}} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{x\\to 0}{e}^{2/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64cb2dc8-7204-5aca-bc9d-3cd91f16892c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264259} {:answer/id #uuid "58beefc3-031f-5cd5-a1cb-88d181ca8560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ebf43-45cc-5265-af34-b7beb51b50f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264261} {:answer/id #uuid "74263b53-7891-5221-9a88-5cdb3a71114a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264262} {:answer/id #uuid "7d70b60a-66f3-5018-8388-0b5c424b7c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d70b60a-66f3-5018-8388-0b5c424b7c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264263} :answer-field/id #uuid "8317f879-b0d4-5e38-b202-437de1124e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6b4e5b-14b3-521d-a29f-b6cecec742c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72336" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382782 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "322bb0f8-d34c-5d73-beef-4d732d5c751f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382784} {:answer/id #uuid "e08c345f-a195-5ac3-9c75-8fb006a80092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382785} {:answer/id #uuid "5b54ea7b-9a7f-56c4-92a9-a7b561334ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382786} {:answer/id #uuid "fa54ee09-bffa-5369-a378-ab3cb86879fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382787} {:answer/id #uuid "732f3e75-c4b6-5940-bff8-1f15ccab93dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "322bb0f8-d34c-5d73-beef-4d732d5c751f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382784} :answer-field/id #uuid "536f7004-0d40-5805-8c75-5fc73256ef67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "053d9468-0f05-5921-951a-24e8dcf2b789" :question/math-academy-id "q-72338" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}+8]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{7}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6{x}^{-7}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
